so the first thing we want to know is  whether consumers seek out Fairtrade  certified products in the marketplace in  order to test that we took Fairtrade  certified coffee we labeled it as  Fairtrade certified in one set of stores  we labeled it with a placebo label that  had no ethical component to it in the  other set of stores and we observed what  happens to sales as consumers are making  their day-to-day purchases in these  stores and what we found was that sales  increased by 10%  a remarkable margin given the  competitive competitiveness of the  industry under the Fair Trade Certified  label the second main finding that came  from the study is that if once we raised  the prices on the Fairtrade certified  coffee sales actually continued to grow  there was a positive price that asta  City but this was only true for one of  two Fairtrade certified coffees that we  tested for the less expensive coffee and  the sales actually decreased  dramatically and so that suggests to us  that there's two segments in the market  there's consumers who are willing to  support the price increases that come  with Fairtrade certification but they're  also consumers who will not support this  and they know it's about an equal split  I think the main takeaway is that their  substantial support for Fairtrade  certification in the marketplace today  but it's not unlimited so I think there  is a fair segment a fairly sizeable  segment that is not willing to pay more  for these ethically made product as much  as we might want to but there are like  there is a fair they lock share of  consumers that are willing to pay more  for these products and that's something  that you know companies I think can take  as an important lesson I mean I think  that this this gives you an opportunity  to differentiate your product from your  competitors and to really like you know  have I think there is a business case  for these ethical differentiation  schemes where you basically try to pass  on some of these costs for ethically  upgrading your supply chains to the  consumers I think the lesson that comes  from our experiment is is that this is  something that you should look into  seriously you should look into whether  you can ethically differentiate your  product cuz there are consumers out  there depending on the market that  you're in that are really willing to  reward you and who might on the other  hand punish you if you don't go down  this road it might be singled out as the  company that's kind of left behind while  other new competitors are ethically  upgrading you're not doing that and and  so I think there's a risk in to not  considering these schemes into not  thinking about you know what can I do to  produce my products in a way that it's  environmentally more friendly that is  sort of ethically more sustainable and  that has a better impact on the planet  which are things that we all care about
 

